.TH FREAD 3S  "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
fread, fwrite \- buffered binary input/output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
#include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B size_t fread(void *\fIptr\fP, size_t \fIsize\fP, 
.B size_t \fInitems\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.PP
.B size_t fwrite(void *\fIptr\fP, size_t \fIsize\fP, 
.B size_t \fInitems\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fread
reads, into a block beginning at
.IR ptr ,
.I nitems
of data of the size of
.I size
from the named input
.IR stream .
It returns the number of items actually read.
.PP
If
.I stream
is
.I stdin
and the standard output is line buffered, then any partial output line
will be flushed before any call to
.IR read (2)
to satisfy the
.IR fread .
.PP
.I Fwrite
appends at most
.I nitems
of data of the size of
.I size
beginning at
.I ptr
to the named output
.IR stream .
It returns the number of items actually written.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
.I Fread
returns the number of elements successfully read, which may be less
than \fInitems\fR if a read error or end-of-file is encountered.  
.PP
.I Fwrite
returns the number of elements successfully written, which may be less
than \fInitems\fR if a write error is encountered.  
.SH "ERRORS"
The underlying function of 
.I fread
is 
.I read.
The underlying function of
.I fwrite
is
.I write.
The errors detected for 
.I fread
and
.I fwrite
are those specified for their respective underlying function.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
read(2),
write(2),
fopen(3S),
getc(3S),
putc(3S),
gets(3S),
puts(3S),
printf(3S),
scanf(3S)
